    {
      "dom_id": "question-213291",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A parodic holiday, International Talk Like a Pirate Day, was celebrated for the first time on September 19th, 1995. Given that January 1st, 1995, was a Sunday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week this holiday was celebrated for the first time.\n\n*Hint: The year 1995 was not a leap year since it is not divisible by $4.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1995, and September 1st, 1995. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July,  and  August).\n\n- There were $2$ months with $30$ days (April and  June).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and September 19th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to September 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(2)+28(1)}}+18.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+2(30)+1(28)+18 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+2(2)+1(0)+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $2$ days after January 1st, 1995, namely Tuesday.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
      "html": "<div id=\"question-213291\" class=\"question\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">16.</span><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"213291\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>A parodic holiday, International Talk Like a Pirate Day, was celebrated for the first time on September 19th, 1995. Given that January 1st, 1995, was a Sunday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week this holiday was celebrated for the first time.</p>\n<p><em>Hint: The year 1995 was not a leap year since it is not divisible by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"242\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4.</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></em></p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyEasy\">E</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Sun, Oct 11th @ 3:59 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:14</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>",
