    {
      "dom_id": "question-212904",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Incorrect",
      "proof_feedback": null,
      "html": "<div id=\"question-212904\" class=\"question\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">19.</span><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"212904\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.</p>\n<p><em>Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"285\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>) and divisible by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"286\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400.</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></em></p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyModerate\">M</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Sun, Oct 11th @ 4:00 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:12</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: red;\">Incorrect</span></span></div></div>",
