    {
      "dom_id": "question-213292",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "On November 16th, 1700, Spain's King Charles II died. Given that January 1st, 1700, was a Friday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week Charles II died.\n\n*Hint: The year 1700 was not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1700, and November 1st, 1700. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 16th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+28(1)}}+15.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(28)+15 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(0)+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $4$ days after January 1st, 1700, namely Tuesday.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
      "html": "<div id=\"question-213292\" class=\"question\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">20.</span><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"213292\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>On November 16th, 1700, Spain's King Charles II died. Given that January 1st, 1700, was a Friday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week Charles II died.</p>\n<p><em>Hint: The year 1700 was not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"299\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>) and is not divisible by <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"300\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400.</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></em></p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyEasy\">E</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Sun, Oct 11th @ 4:00 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:12</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>",
