      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14116827/observations/lesson-step-129887-3c67bdfb78409eb9f1c2.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14116827/observations/lesson-step-129887-3c67bdfb78409eb9f1c2.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/2673",
      "captured_at": 1791716510.7183776,
      "math_academy_id": 9013,
      "title": "Integers Congruent to Zero",
      "content": "According to the definition of modular congruence, we have that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = b+ kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Substituting $b=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid a  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nThis gives rise to the following important fact:\n\n*Any integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n.$*\n\nFor example, the numbers $10,$ $-25,$ and $75$ are all are all multiples of ${\\color{blue}5},$ so they are all congruent to $0$ modulo ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ -25 & =(-5)\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 75 & =15\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-129891",
      "name": "Simplifying Congruences Using Integers Congruent to Zero",
      "problem": "Let's try to simplify the right-hand side of the following congruence:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 102 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nBy \"simplify,\" we mean that we wish to transform the number on the right-hand side to be the smallest possible positive integer. In other words, we want to write\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < 5.\n$$\n\nWhen we add $0$ to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing $0$ as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:{\\color{black}n}).$ \n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}5} \\cdot (-20) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}5})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡102\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x+0 & ≡102+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & \\text{ }≡102+5\\cdot (-20)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡102-100\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x \\equiv 102\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ is equivalent to $x \\equiv 2\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).$\n\nOur answer is correct because $102$ and $2$ both have a remainder of $2$ when divided by $5,$ and $0 \\leq  2 < 5.$\n\n**Note:** By the division algorithm, there is exactly one value of $r$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < n.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
