      "math_academy_id": 9123,
      "title": "Proof of the Addition and Subtraction Properties",
      "content": "In this lesson, we've been working with the so-called addition property of modular arithmetic.\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the addition property by writing $a\\equiv b$ in terms of divisibility:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)-(b+c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)≡(b+c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe subtraction property is proved similarly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)-(b-c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)≡(b-c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
  ],
